WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
The gas in this camping stove is mostly

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.520
butane, which boils near minus one degree.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.840
The liquid in this bottle is ethanol.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.840
Ethanol molecules have fewer electrons than butane, so

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.800
you'd expect weaker dispersion forces and a lower

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:15.600
boiling point.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.600
Instead, ethanol boils at about seventy eight degrees.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.960
And it mixes completely with water, while butane

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.360
barely dissolves.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.200
The difference is one O H group.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:29.760
So how does an O H group change

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.200
a molecule's properties?

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.560
On paper, take ethane and swap one hydrogen

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.840
for an O H group.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.400
That gives ethanol, an alcohol.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.600
This O H is a hydroxyl group, on

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.000
a carbon with only single bonds.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.200
It's covalently bonded, so it isn't a hydroxide

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:49.680
ion.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.360
Inside the molecule, oxygen pulls the shared electrons

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.360
more strongly than hydrogen does.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.160
So the O H bond is polar: the

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.920
oxygen is slightly negative, the hydrogen slightly positive.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.440
And the oxygen keeps two lone pairs, electrons

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:06.880
not used in bonding.

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.620
Naming uses the rules you already know, with

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:12.780
one change.

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.380
Find the longest chain that holds the carbon

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:16.660
with the O H.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.700
Take the alkane's name, drop its final e,

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:20.980
and add ol.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.940
Number from the end nearer the O H,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.500
and put that number before ol.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:29.620
So C H three, C H two, C

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.660
H two O H is propan-one-ol.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.300
Move the O H to the middle carbon,

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.140
and it's propan-two-ol.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.340
Methanol and ethanol need no number, because there's

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.500
only one place the O H can go.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.660
With a branch as well, the O H

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:49.140
wins the numbering, like a double bond, because

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.660
it's named in the ending.

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.140
In this chain of five, numbering from the

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.020
left puts the O H on carbon two,

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.420
and the methyl on carbon four: four-methylpentan-two-ol.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.060
From the right, the methyl would get a

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.020
two, but the O H would get a

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.060
four, so that name is wrong.

00:02:09.067 --> 00:02:11.706
Now find the carbon holding the O H,

00:02:11.867 --> 00:02:14.506
and count the carbons bonded directly to it.

00:02:14.827 --> 00:02:18.106
One carbon makes a primary alcohol, like ethanol.

00:02:18.347 --> 00:02:21.946
Two carbons make it secondary, like propan-two-ol.

00:02:22.147 --> 00:02:26.106
Three make it tertiary, like two-methylpropan-two-ol.

00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:29.946
Count only carbons, not hydrogens or the oxygen.

00:02:30.187 --> 00:02:32.586
Methanol has no other carbon at all, but

00:02:32.627 --> 00:02:34.106
it's counted as primary.

00:02:36.134 --> 00:02:38.346
Don't judge the class by how branched a

00:02:38.374 --> 00:02:39.533
molecule looks.

00:02:39.814 --> 00:02:43.253
Two-methylpropan-one-ol has a branch, but its O H

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:46.053
carbon is bonded to just one other carbon,

00:02:46.134 --> 00:02:47.213
so it's primary.

00:02:47.494 --> 00:02:50.213
Always go to the O H carbon first,

00:02:50.334 --> 00:02:51.093
then count.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.780
Back to the boiling point puzzle.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.620
In liquid ethanol, the slightly positive hydrogen of

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.260
one O H is attracted to a lone

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.820
pair on the oxygen of a neighbouring molecule.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:05.820
That's a hydrogen bond.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.020
The hydrogen is the donor, and the oxygen's

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:10.700
lone pair is the acceptor.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:13.740
It's still far weaker than a covalent bond.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:17.820
Alcohols also have dispersion forces from their chains,

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:18.940
like alkanes.

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:22.566
So compare a fair pair.

00:03:22.847 --> 00:03:26.566
Propane and ethanol each have twenty six electrons,

00:03:26.647 --> 00:03:28.646
so their dispersion forces are similar.

00:03:29.287 --> 00:03:32.006
Propane boils at minus forty two degrees.

00:03:32.487 --> 00:03:35.446
Ethanol boils at seventy eight, about one hundred

00:03:35.527 --> 00:03:37.166
and twenty degrees higher.

00:03:37.447 --> 00:03:39.926
The extra comes from hydrogen bonding.

00:03:40.247 --> 00:03:43.846
Boiling overcomes both attractions between the molecules, but

00:03:43.927 --> 00:03:46.166
the O H bond inside each one stays

00:03:46.247 --> 00:03:46.966
intact.

00:03:48.967 --> 00:03:51.606
Along the series, each of these alcohols has

00:03:51.687 --> 00:03:54.406
one O H, so the hydrogen bonding is

00:03:54.527 --> 00:03:55.486
about the same.

00:03:55.767 --> 00:03:57.846
What changes is the chain.

00:03:58.167 --> 00:04:01.446
Methanol boils at sixty five degrees, ethanol at

00:04:01.487 --> 00:04:05.766
seventy eight, propan-one-ol at ninety seven, and butan-one-ol

00:04:05.847 --> 00:04:07.846
at one hundred and eighteen.

00:04:07.927 --> 00:04:10.486
Each extra C H two adds electrons and

00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:13.766
contact, so the dispersion forces grow, and the

00:04:13.847 --> 00:04:15.766
boiling point climbs steadily.

00:04:17.767 --> 00:04:19.526
Now hold the formula fixed.

00:04:20.007 --> 00:04:24.966
Butan-one-ol, butan-two-ol and two-methylpropan-two-ol are

00:04:25.047 --> 00:04:27.926
all C four H ten O, and all

00:04:28.087 --> 00:04:30.286
three hydrogen bond about equally.

00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:33.046
But their boiling points fall: one hundred and

00:04:33.127 --> 00:04:37.126
eighteen, about one hundred, then eighty two degrees.

00:04:37.207 --> 00:04:39.526
The tertiary one is the most compact, like

00:04:39.607 --> 00:04:42.406
a ball, so it touches its neighbours less,

00:04:42.447 --> 00:04:44.886
and its dispersion forces are weaker.

00:04:44.967 --> 00:04:48.486
So for isomers, primary usually boils highest and

00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:51.153
tertiary lowest, but it's the shape doing the

00:04:51.207 --> 00:04:51.686
work.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.700
Solubility comes down to the same O H.

00:04:56.980 --> 00:05:00.020
Water molecules hold each other with hydrogen bonds.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.140
Hexane couldn't replace those, so it stayed as

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:03.940
a layer.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.460
An alcohol's O H can: it hydrogen bonds

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:10.420
with water, replacing the bonds it breaks.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.340
So methanol, ethanol and propan-one-ol mix with water

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.700
in any proportion.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:20.100
But the non-polar chain still has to push

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:23.540
in between water molecules, breaking hydrogen bonds it

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:24.500
can't replace.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.620
The longer the chain, the more it breaks,

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.540
and one O H can't make up for

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:29.780
it.

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.340
So solubility drops gradually.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:37.780
Butan-one-ol dissolves partly, and hexan-one-ol barely dissolves,

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.620
so it forms a layer on top.

00:05:41.634 --> 00:05:44.273
So, the O H group gives an alcohol

00:05:44.354 --> 00:05:45.553
hydrogen bonds.

00:05:45.794 --> 00:05:47.953
That's why it boils higher than an alkane

00:05:47.994 --> 00:05:50.833
of the same size, and small alcohols mix

00:05:50.914 --> 00:05:52.033
with water.

00:05:52.114 --> 00:05:54.913
Then the chain sets the trend: longer chains

00:05:54.994 --> 00:05:57.313
boil higher, but dissolve less.

00:05:57.554 --> 00:05:58.473
Same size?

00:05:58.674 --> 00:06:00.353
Hydrogen bonding wins.

00:06:00.434 --> 00:06:01.553
Same family?

00:06:01.634 --> 00:06:02.913
Size wins.

00:06:02.954 --> 00:06:05.153
And to classify, go to the O H

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:07.153
carbon, and count its carbons.

00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:09.806
Your turn.

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:13.406
An alcohol has the condensed formula C H

00:06:13.487 --> 00:06:17.006
three, C H O H, C H C

00:06:17.167 --> 00:06:19.606
H three, C H three.

00:06:19.967 --> 00:06:22.526
Give its IUPAC name, and classify it as

00:06:22.607 --> 00:06:25.006
primary, secondary or tertiary.

00:06:25.487 --> 00:06:27.086
Justify your classification.

00:06:27.647 --> 00:06:29.006
Pause here to answer.

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:42.953
The longest chain has four carbons and holds

00:06:43.034 --> 00:06:45.593
the O H carbon, so it's a butanol.

00:06:46.074 --> 00:06:48.713
Number from the left, nearer the O H:

00:06:48.794 --> 00:06:50.953
it's on carbon two, with the methyl on

00:06:51.034 --> 00:06:51.833
carbon three.

00:06:52.314 --> 00:06:54.553
So it's three-methylbutan-two-ol.

00:06:55.034 --> 00:06:56.953
The O H carbon is bonded to two

00:06:57.034 --> 00:06:59.113
other carbons, so it's secondary.

00:06:59.354 --> 00:07:02.953
Two-methylbutan-three-ol numbers for the branch, but the O

00:07:03.034 --> 00:07:03.993
H wins.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:06.640
Last one.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.040
Butane boils at minus one degree.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.440
Propan-one-ol boils at ninety seven degrees.

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.520
Both molecules have thirty four electrons.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.520
Explain the difference in boiling points, with reference

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.880
to the intramolecular and intermolecular bonding present.

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:24.720
Pause here to answer.

00:07:35.934 --> 00:07:39.133
Both have thirty four electrons, so their dispersion

00:07:39.214 --> 00:07:40.253
forces are similar.

00:07:40.734 --> 00:07:44.013
But propan-one-ol has a polar O H bond,

00:07:44.094 --> 00:07:46.813
so its molecules also form hydrogen bonds with

00:07:46.894 --> 00:07:48.973
each other, which butane can't.

00:07:49.614 --> 00:07:52.093
More energy is needed to separate them, so

00:07:52.254 --> 00:07:53.293
it boils higher.

00:07:53.934 --> 00:07:56.973
Boiling leaves the covalent bonds inside both molecules

00:07:57.054 --> 00:07:58.013
intact.

00:07:58.334 --> 00:08:01.853
Just writing "propan-one-ol is polar" isn't enough.

00:08:02.334 --> 00:08:03.533
Name the force.

00:08:05.534 --> 00:08:08.573
So one O H group adds hydrogen bonding,

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:10.973
and the chain decides how much the dispersion

00:08:11.054 --> 00:08:12.133
forces add.

00:08:12.574 --> 00:08:16.413
Next: how we actually make ethanol, from ethene,

00:08:16.454 --> 00:08:19.533
from a haloalkane, or from sugar and yeast.

00:08:21.534 --> 00:08:22.973
Here are the key notes.

00:08:23.214 --> 00:08:25.133
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:25.214 --> 00:08:26.013
to copy them.
